\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Complex Spin in dimension six is #{SL_4} on a half-spin module}

\p{Let #{V=W\oplus W'} be a split nondegenerate complex quadratic
space with #{\dim W=3}. For polar-dual bases #{e_i,e'_i}, use

##{S^+:(1,e_{12},e_{13},e_{23}),\qquad
S^-:(e_{123},-e_3,e_2,-e_1).}

For #{\langle s,t\rangle=[e_{123}]\bigl(
\operatorname{rev}_{\wedge}(s)\wedge t\bigr)}, the second displayed
basis is dual to the first, and the full pairing matrix on
#{S^+\oplus S^-} is

##{\Omega=\begin{pmatrix}0&I_4\\-I_4&0\end{pmatrix}.}

Clifford generators are self-adjoint for this pairing. Under the paired
even-algebra equivalence #{\Phi} of \ref{fcap-002V}, reversal therefore sends

##{(A,D)\longmapsto(D^{\mathsf T},A^{\mathsf T}).}

Thus the even reverse-norm-one group is

##{\{(A,A^{-\mathsf T}):A\in GL_4(\mathbb C)\}.}

At this stage it is #{GL_4}, not yet the actual Spin group.}

\p{The vector-normalization condition cuts out the determinant-one
subgroup. If
#{v=\sum_i x_i e_i+y_i e'_i}, its odd Clifford action has block form

##{\rho(v)=\begin{pmatrix}0&B_v\\B_v^\sharp&0\end{pmatrix},}

where

##{B_v=\begin{pmatrix}
0&-y_3&y_2&-y_1\\
y_3&0&x_1&x_2\\
-y_2&-x_1&0&x_3\\
y_1&-x_2&-x_3&0
\end{pmatrix}.}

These matrices run through all skew #{4\times4} matrices. For a skew
matrix #{B}, define #{B^\sharp} by

##{\begin{aligned}
(B^\sharp)_{01}&=B_{23},&
(B^\sharp)_{02}&=-B_{13},&
(B^\sharp)_{03}&=B_{12},\\
(B^\sharp)_{12}&=B_{03},&
(B^\sharp)_{13}&=-B_{02},&
(B^\sharp)_{23}&=B_{01},
\end{aligned}}

with skew extension. Then
#{(B^\sharp)^\sharp=B},
#{BB^\sharp=-\operatorname{Pf}(B)I}, and
#{\operatorname{Pf}(B_v)=-\sum_i x_i y_i}. The last two identities give
#{\rho(v)^2=Q(v)I}, matching the Clifford convention.}

\p{The complementary-minor identity is

##{(ABA^{\mathsf T})^\sharp
=\det(A)A^{-\mathsf T}B^\sharp A^{-1}.}

One derivation identifies a skew matrix with an alternating two-form:
the operation #{B\mapsto B^\sharp} is the complementary two-form determined
by the ordered volume basis, while #{\bigwedge^4 A} multiplies that
volume by #{\det A}. Evaluating on basis pairs gives the six coordinate
rules above and the displayed identity.}

\p{Conjugation by #{\operatorname{diag}(A,A^{-\mathsf T})} sends the
two off-diagonal blocks to

##{ABA^{\mathsf T},\qquad
A^{-\mathsf T}B^\sharp A^{-1}.}

By the complementary-minor identity, it preserves the vector graph
#{\{(B,B^\sharp):B^{\mathsf T}=-B\}} exactly when
#{\det A=1}. Sufficiency is immediate. For necessity, choose any nonzero
#{B^\sharp}; equality of the two lower blocks forces
#{\det A=1}. Faithfulness of the full Clifford action in this even
dimension lifts this matrix statement to normalization of the actual
vector subspace.}

\p{Every Spin element therefore acts on #{S^+} through
#{SL_4(\mathbb C)}. Conversely, for #{A\in SL_4(\mathbb C)}, let
#{x} be the unique even Clifford preimage of
#{(A,A^{-\mathsf T})}. It has reverse norm one and, by the graph
calculation, normalizes #{V}. The carrier-completion argument of
\ref{fcap-0032} places #{x} in actual Spin. The mutually inverse maps are

##{g\longmapsto\rho_+(g),\qquad
A\longmapsto\Phi^{-1}(A,A^{-\mathsf T}).}

Hence

##{\Spin_6(\mathbb C)\simeq SL_4(\mathbb C).}

In the displayed bases #{S^+} is the standard four-dimensional module
and #{S^-} is its dual.}

\p{\citet{Exercise 20.39, p. 311}{fulton2004representation} give the
group and standard #{S^+} module.
\citet{Section 6.3.3, Exercise 1, p. 322}{goodman2009symmetry}
independently give the same complex group. Here the cross-pairing proves
#{S^-} is dual, and the vector-graph calculation proves the actual image.}

\p{\citet{Section 3.7.6, p. 84}{meinrenken2013clifford} gives the
compact real form #{\Spin(6)=SU(4)}.}

\p{TauCeti formalizes the paired half-spin algebra and matrix equivalences
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivProdEnd}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivProdMatrix}.
The vector graph, determinant-one group image and inverse are proved above,
not by these declarations.}
